---

The `ogygia` package is the region system: islands, lakes, holes, held regions, the SPA router and the server handle. Every other feature is a separate package, with its own section in these docs. You install only what you use, so an app that never reads a flag carries none of the flag code.

| Package                                           | Plugin                           | What it is for                                                                                         |
| ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| [`@ogygia/content`](/docs/content/overview)       | `content({ markdown, presets })` | Content collections, markdown with Shiki, the docs and blog shells, search.                            |
| [`@ogygia/router`](/docs/router/overview)         | `router()`                       | The server router: pages, layouts, loads, actions and endpoints as one typed table.                    |
| [`@ogygia/postrender`](/docs/postrender/overview) | `postrender({ ttl, default })`   | Pages stored whole on their first request and served as bytes.                                         |
| [`@ogygia/devtools`](/docs/devtools/overview)     | `devtools({ dock, profiler })`   | The in-page dock and the SSR profiler.                                                                 |
| [`@ogygia/testing`](/docs/testing/overview)       | none                             | The devtools and the profiler as test primitives, for Playwright or any runner.                        |
| [`@ogygia/flags`](/docs/flags/overview)           | `flags()`                        | Kill switches, rollouts and A/B tests, decided on the server.                                          |
| [`@ogygia/federation`](/docs/federation/overview) | `federation()`                   | Other teams' apps rendered into your pages, as signed regions.                                         |
| [`@ogygia/cli`](/docs/cli/overview)               | none                             | The `ogygia` command: `init`, the AI skill and MCP server, perf budgets, federation keys.              |
| [`@ogygia/core`](/docs/core/overview)             | none                             | The shared core that `ogygia` re-exports and every plugin builds on. You install it only to write one. |

Every package takes `@ogygia/core` as a peer dependency, which `ogygia` already brings in. They all release together under one version number, so keep them on the same version as `ogygia`.

One more package is for library authors rather than apps. [`@ogygia/tsdown-plugin-svelte`](/docs/tsdown-plugin-svelte/overview) ships a library's Svelte components from a tsdown build, the way `svelte-package` does. It works for any Svelte library, has no dependency on ogygia and its own version number, and it is what ogygia's own packages are built with.

## Turn a feature on

Install the package, then list its plugin once in the `plugins` array of your Vite config, beside `ogygia()`. Each plugin is a Vite plugin in its own right, and `ogygia()` finds it in that list. Each factory is imported from `<package>/vite`:

::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npm i @ogygia/flags @ogygia/postrender
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm add @ogygia/flags @ogygia/postrender
```

:::

```ts title=vite.config.ts
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { flags } from '@ogygia/flags/vite';
import { postrender } from '@ogygia/postrender/vite';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    ogygia(),
    flags(),
    postrender({ ttl: 600 }),
    sveltekit()
  ]
});
```

`ogygia()` comes first, the feature plugins follow it, and `sveltekit()` goes last. `ogygia.handle()` in `hooks.server.ts` already runs the server half of every listed plugin, and the page runtime boots every browser half. What the Vite config can't carry is anything live or app-specific: a flag vendor, your federation identity, your route table, a store client. Most packages therefore also ask for a line or two in `hooks.server.ts`, and each package's overview says which.

These rules hold for every plugin:

- **Call the factory.** Write `flags()`, not `flags`; the build tells you if you forget.
- **List each plugin once.** A plugin listed twice is a build error.
- **Only alongside `ogygia()`.** A plugin listed without `ogygia()` fails the build with an explanation. The old `plugins` option of `ogygia()` has been removed, and passing it is a build error that names the new form.
- **Options are plain JSON.** They reach the plugin's server half at run time. Live objects (a store client, a vendor SDK) are set up in `hooks.server.ts` through the package's own API.
- **List it only where you want it.** The list is code, so a plugin can depend on the mode: `...(mode !== 'production' ? [devtools()] : [])`.

## Several packages in one app

When several packages need a handle, the order in the `sequence()` is postrender first, then ogygia, then the router. A stored page is served before anything renders, and the route table answers inside the request ogygia has already prepared:

```ts title=src/hooks.server.ts
import { sequence } from '@sveltejs/kit/hooks';
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia/server';
import { postrender } from '@ogygia/postrender';
import { router } from '$lib/router';

export const handle = sequence(postrender.handle(), ogygia.handle(), router.handle);
```

The packages also read from each other without any wiring of yours. A flag read marks the render as per-visitor, so postrender never stores one variant of a test. A route table sets postrender's `config` key per page. A federated fragment is a region, so a publish on another team clears the stored pages that baked it. Each package's overview has a "with other packages" part that shows these.

To write a package of your own on the same API, see [Writing a plugin](/docs/core/plugins).
